# Test: a peer created from a dynamic group advertises the RFC 9234 Role
# capability its GROUP states.
#
# VALIDATES: a dynamic group's `role` block reaches the capability injector under
#            the group's own selector (extractPeerRoleConfigs, through
#            configjson.ForEachPeer's template visit and
#            configjson.CapabilitySelector), and the member built from that group
#            resolves it (Peer.getPluginCapabilities,
#            internal/component/bgp/reactor/peer.go, third link of its fallback
#            chain, gated on PeerSettings.IsDynamic).
# PREVENTS: an IXP route server whose members advertise NO Role capability.
#           bgp-role reads its config through configjson.ForEachPeer, which
#           visited `bgp.peer` and `bgp.group.<name>.peer` only. A dynamic group
#           carries no `peer` list, so it was skipped whole and its name was
#           discarded by the loop. The operator wrote `role import rs` on the one
#           object a listen-range group has, `ze config validate` accepted it, and
#           nothing advertised it. RFC 9234's whole Section 5 machinery is keyed
#           off the negotiated pair, so a route server silently ran with none of
#           it against exactly the peer population an operator has not vetted.
#
# DISCRIMINATION: the assertion is ze's OPEN, whole, and the three octets 09 01 01
# at its tail are the subject. Measured 2026-08-13 by deleting the group link from
# getPluginCapabilities: the OPEN arrives 3 octets shorter (length 002B, optional
# parameter length 0E) with no capability 9 at all, and this file goes red on the
# hex. It cannot pass vacuously either: 01 is the STATED value (RFC 9234 Table 1,
# RS), not a default and not the zero byte a miss would leave -- 0 is Provider,
# and a peer with no role config gets no capability 9 at all rather than a zero.
#
# The peer takes the ACTIVE role and dials ze (--dial): the group is `ip dynamic`
# + `connect false`, so ze only ever ACCEPTS. The configuration names NO static
# peer, so the only socket in the run is the one the GROUP asks for and the only
# peer is the one the inbound connection creates (tryCreateDynamicPeer,
# internal/component/bgp/reactor/reactor_dynamic.go).
#
# NOT covered here, and covered elsewhere: the RFC 9234 Section 5 OTC gates and
# Section 4.2 strict mode DO run for this member. getFilterConfig falls back to
# PeerFilterInfo.GroupName, and applyValidateOpen (role.go) reads the group from
# rpc.ValidateOpenInput.Group, which internal/component/bgp/server/validate.go
# fills. Both are proven at the unit level, by TestOTCIngressGateRunsForADynamic\
# GroupMember and TestValidateOpenStrictModeRefusesADynamicGroupMemberWithNoRole
# (role/dynamic_group_test.go). This file stays on what the member ADVERTISES,
# because that is the one link with no unit-test seam: the reactor's capability
# injector is reachable only from a real plugin process.

# "dynamic peer created" is an Info line and the daemon's base level is warn
# (ze.log, internal/core/slogutil/slogutil.go), so the assertion at the foot of
# this file sees nothing without this.
option=env:var=ze.log.bgp.reactor:value=info

stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=asn:value=65010
option=tcp_connections:value=1

# ze-peer mirrors ze's Role capability by default. RS's complement is RS-Client
# (RFC 9234 Section 4.2, Table 2), so the pair is stated explicitly rather than
# left to a mirror: this file must not depend on bgp-role finding no config for a
# dynamic peer and accepting the OPEN for that reason.
option=open:value=drop-capability:code=9
option=open:value=add-capability:code=9:hex=02

# Hand ze's OPEN to the checker as message 1 of the exchange.
option=open:value=inspect-open-message

# ze's OPEN, whole. Version 4, my AS 65533, hold time 30 (001E, from the group's
# timer block), BGP identifier 1.2.3.4, then one RFC 5492 type-2 optional
# parameter (length 0F) holding three capabilities:
#
#   01 04 0001 00 01   Multiprotocol ipv4/unicast (RFC 4760 Section 8)
#   41 04 0000FFFD     4-octet AS (RFC 6793)
#   09 01 01           Role = RS (RFC 9234 Section 4.1, Table 1)
#
# The Role capability is last because session_negotiate.go appends the plugin
# getter's answer after the multiprotocol and ASN4 capabilities it builds itself.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF002E0104FFFD001E0102030411020F01040001000141040000FFFD090101

# End-of-RIB for ipv4/unicast (RFC 4724 Section 2). Its arrival says the session
# reached initial sync, so the OPEN above was the start of a real session rather
# than a handshake that died.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=2:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
EOF_PEER

# One dynamic group and nothing else: the route-server shape. The `role` block is
# stated at the group level, which is where an IXP states its member policy.
# tmpfs rather than stdin because the runner writes daemon.pid and daemon.ready
# beside the config, and the compiled trigger waits on both.
tmpfs=ze-bgp.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
bgp {
	# A declared RFC 9234 role requires each bound chain to name a filter that
	# can refuse a path through a transit provider (RFC 7454 Section 9). What
	# this test exercises is elsewhere, so the decision is recorded and the
	# filter is left deactivated: the chain names it, and it never runs.
	policy {
		reject-asn NO-TRANSIT {
			indirect [ 3356 ]
		}
	}
	filter {
		import [ NO-TRANSIT ]
		export [ NO-TRANSIT ]
		inactive: import NO-TRANSIT
		inactive: export NO-TRANSIT
	}
	group ix {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip dynamic
				connect false
				range 127.0.0.0/8
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept true
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65533
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast {
					mode enable
					prefix {
						maximum 10000
					}
				}
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		timer {
			receive-hold-time 30
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}

		# The subject. Strict mode is NOT set: it is enforced in
		# validateOpenRolePair, which resolves config through
		# rpc.ValidateOpenInput and so cannot reach a dynamic member's group.
		# Setting it here would assert a hole rather than this feature.
		role {
			import rs
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# Trigger: let the session establish and drain, then stop the daemon. The sleep
# only has to cover a dial the peer retries every 500ms against a daemon that has
# already written daemon.ready; too short and the file fails on the missing
# End-of-RIB rather than passing quietly.

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --dial 127.0.0.1:$PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=background:seq=2:exec=ze-test fixture plugin/dynamic-peer-gets-group-role-capability-trigger
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=ze --plugin ze.bgp-role start ze-bgp.conf:timeout=30s

# The session above really was a DYNAMIC peer's. Without this the wire assertions
# would hold for any peer ze accepted, which is how a first attempt at a dynamic
# test can pass against unfixed code: the inbound is attributed to a statically
# configured peer and no dynamic peer is ever built.
expect=stderr:contains=dynamic peer created

# ... and it reached Established, so the End-of-RIB above was sent by the initial
# sync of a live session rather than by any earlier state.
expect=stderr:contains=session established
